/sdlc decide spec v2 batch
```json
[
  {
    "action": "decide",
    "questionId": "Q-1",
    "fields": {
      "Outcome": "Each readiness request must perform a fresh, minimal read-only query against the actual ticket schema through the same configured application database used by ticket operations, including an already-initialized shared store. Opening a connection or executing SELECT 1 alone is insufficient. A successfully queried empty, initialized tickets table is ready. Do not write business records, probe a substitute database, or reuse a prior successful result. This establishes database-read capability only; initialization and write limitations are addressed separately in Q-2 and Q-6.",
      "Rationale": "This is the smallest useful increment that distinguishes a live process from an unreadable ticket dependency and detects a query or schema failure after earlier success without introducing write-probe side effects."
    }
  },
  {
    "action": "decide",
    "questionId": "Q-2",
    "fields": {
      "Outcome": "Allow readiness to invoke the existing normal lazy initialization of the configured TicketStore before its read-only query. Existing directory creation, WAL setup, schema creation, owner-column migration, and seeding of a genuinely new database may occur exactly as they do today. Do not reset, reseed, or update existing ticket records or timestamps, and do not seed an intentionally empty existing tickets table. Initialization failures produce the same unavailable readiness response as query failures. Read-only describes the probe after normal initialization, not a promise that the first request is entirely side-effect-free.",
      "Rationale": "Preserving the established initialization boundary supports fresh instances and pre-owner databases without adding a separate startup mechanism or breaking accepted business-data preservation."
    }
  },
  {
    "action": "decide",
    "questionId": "Q-3",
    "fields": {
      "Outcome": "Use GET /api/ready with exactly 200 and {\"status\":\"ready\"} on success, or 503 and {\"status\":\"unavailable\"} on database initialization or query failure. Both states use application/json and Cache-Control: no-store. The failure token is unavailable, not the original proposal's unready, to match the existing tools/brownfield-demo/probes.mjs consumer contract. HEAD performs the same current dependency check and returns matching status and headers with no body. Other methods return 405 with Allow: GET, HEAD and do not initialize or probe the database. Ignore query parameters and any GET body; accept no caller-provided database path, SQL, or configuration overrides. Responses disclose only the generic state. Preserve GET /api/health as 200 with {\"status\":\"ok\"}, independent of database access.",
      "Rationale": "An exact, non-cacheable contract is easy to validate and compatible with the repository's existing reliability consumers. Fixed server configuration and sanitized responses prevent caller inputs or database diagnostics from changing or leaking through the probe."
    }
  },
  {
    "action": "decide",
    "questionId": "Q-4",
    "fields": {
      "Outcome": "For each readiness initialization or query failure, emit one fixed generic server-side readiness-failure error event. Do not include the raw exception, message, stack, database path, ticket contents, credentials, request inputs, or other dynamic sensitive values. Do not add transition tracking, stateful result caching, or a new logging dependency. This increment has no numeric endpoint-latency or SQLite-lock deadline requirement and does not change database busy-timeout semantics. Treat this as an explicitly accepted limitation, not a measured responsiveness guarantee: synchronous SQLite contention can delay readiness and other requests. Follow-up owner: huangyingting; before claiming production readiness or bounded traffic-admission latency, measure the supported runtime under representative lock contention and separately specify any required timeout or execution isolation.",
      "Rationale": "A fixed diagnostic surfaces failures without leaking details or adding state. No latency baseline exists, so inventing a deadline would create an unsupported promise and expand this small demo increment. The deferred performance risk remains visible with an owner and concrete follow-up."
    }
  },
  {
    "action": "decide",
    "questionId": "Q-5",
    "fields": {
      "Outcome": "Acceptance must use the delivered immutable image and disposable isolated resources to observe healthy, invalid-database-path, and repaired states through actual HTTP requests. A faulty configuration is repaired by recreating the instance; dynamic database-path changes within a running process are not required. Retain one populated isolated application volume through healthy restart and repaired recreation and compare pre-existing records and timestamps, allowing only intentional user edits. Verify repeated readiness checks, DB-independent liveness, dashboard and detail reads, ticket creation, assign/reassign/clear, status changes, and intersecting filters. Also require deterministic isolated coverage showing that the warm shared store returns unavailable when its ticket-schema query fails after earlier success and becomes ready when that query succeeds again, without cached success or permanent failure. Preserve fresh-database, empty-existing-table, and pre-owner-schema initialization coverage. The additional lock-contention, corrupt-file, and read-only write-failure matrices are outside this increment; do not claim they were exercised or solved.",
      "Rationale": "Configuration repair by recreation matches the existing process-scoped database configuration. Live HTTP and populated-volume evidence validate the real symptom and preservation guarantees; warm-store failure and recovery coverage validates the new per-request behavior. A broader fault matrix is unnecessary for the first bounded increment."
    }
  },
  {
    "action": "decide",
    "questionId": "Q-6",
    "fields": {
      "Outcome": "Accept explicitly limited database-read readiness under /api/ready. A ready response establishes only that normal initialization and the current ticket-schema read succeeded; it does not guarantee writable storage, successful ticket mutations, all application journeys, authentication, latency, production fitness, or sufficient grounds for production traffic admission. Demonstrate real write operations during isolated recovery acceptance, but do not perform writes on every probe. The subsequent reliability exercise must combine readiness with the relevant application journey and its own admission policy, and must not describe this endpoint alone as full application readiness.",
      "Rationale": "This is consistent with the read-only probe chosen in Q-1 and avoids a misleading write guarantee. Keeping the existing consumer-compatible endpoint while explicitly documenting its limits delivers useful evidence without inflating scope or certifying production behavior."
    }
  }
]
```

